{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ovps6l7z46ipc6vshf5lkru3f75lm6wfm3hodmdtko7zcczxrh4q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01HeXZnFaUy7BgPVe1h3oPcv","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Audit C vs Rust for divergences","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Do a careful, line-by-line semantic audit of a C-to-Rust translation. Report ONLY concrete divergences (or confirm none). Do NOT edit any file.\n\nFiles (absolute paths):\n- C ground truth: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-6M6KAs/c_src/src/lib.c  (963 lines, single TU, an inlined copy of the stb_ds.h implementation plus `strkey` and `helxo`)\n- Rust translation: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-6M6KAs/translation/src/lib.rs (1247 lines)\n\nRead BOTH files in full. For each C function, find its Rust counterpart and compare semantics EXACTLY. The following functions matter:\nstbds_arrgrowf, stbds_arrfreef, stbds_rand_seed, stbds_probe_position, stbds_log2,\nstbds_make_hash_index, stbds_hash_string, stbds_siphash_bytes, stbds_hash_bytes,\nstbds_is_key_equal, stbds_hmfree_func, stbds_hm_find_slot, stbds_hmget_key_ts,\nstbds_hmget_key, stbds_hmput_default, stbds_hmput_key, stbds_shmode_func,\nstbds_hmdel_key, stbds_strdup, stbds_stralloc, stbds_strreset, strkey, helxo.\n\nPay SPECIFIC attention to these classes of bug, since the rest is already verified by a passing differential test suite:\n\n1. Integer promotion / signedness: C expressions like `d[0] | (d[1]<<8) | (d[2]<<16) | (d[3]<<24)` have type `int` and sign-extend when assigned to `size_t`. Check every such site in `stbds_siphash_bytes` (both the body loop at lib.c:522-530 and the fall-through `switch` at lib.c:531-541) against the Rust. Also check the macro `stbds_load_32_or_64` (lib.c:360-363) as used at lib.c:410-411 — which operands are `int` vs `unsigned int` in C, and does the Rust widen identically?\n2. Order of evaluation / assignment chains, e.g. lib.c:708 `stbds_header(a)->hash_table = table = nt;` and lib.c:786-788 `stbds_temp_key(a) = *(char**)(...) = stbds_strdup(...)`.\n3. Statement-level fall-through in the `switch (len - i)` (lib.c:532) and the non-fall-through `switch (table->string.mode)` (lib.c:785).\n4. `>=` vs `==` comparisons on `mode`: lib.c:560, 590, 713 use `mode >= STBDS_HM_STRING`, but lib.c:836 and lib.c:842 use `mode == STBDS_HM_STRING`. Confirm the Rust preserves each one exactly.\n5. Truncating casts: lib.c:803 `h->string.mode = (unsigned char) mode;` and lib.c:825 `int i = slot & STBDS_BUCKET_MASK;`.\n6. Wrapping vs panicking arithmetic: any place the C relies on unsigned wraparound (e.g. `2 * stbds_arrcap(a)`, `stbds_hash_seed * a + b`, `len << (STBDS_SIZE_T_BITS-8)`, `hash += 2`, `--table->tombstone_count`) where the Rust uses a non-wrapping operator that could panic in debug builds.\n7. Shift amounts: `STBDS_ROTATE_LEFT/RIGHT` (lib.c:474-475) and `512u << (a->block>>1)` at lib.c:888 — could the Rust shift by >= 64 and panic where the C is merely UB-but-benign?\n8. Any C statement with NO Rust counterpart at all, or vice versa.\n9. `helxo` (lib.c:945-963): the C does `printf(\"%s %c\\n\", hash[z], hash[z].value)` passing a 16-byte STRUCT `{char* key; char value;}` by value as the first variadic argument. Verify the Rust's reproduction is ABI-equivalent on x86-64 SysV.\n\nFor every divergence report: the C line number, the exact C expression, the Rust line number, the exact Rust expression, and a concrete input that would make them differ. If a construct looks different but is provably equivalent, say \"equivalent\" and give the one-line reason. Be precise and terse; no summaries of what the code does."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4638,"cache_read_input_tokens":203584,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4638,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier"