 and lib.c:786-788 `stbds_temp_key(a) = *(char**)(...) = stbds_strdup(...)`.\n3. Statement-level fall-through in the `switch (len - i)` (lib.c:532) and the non-fall-through `switch (table->string.mode)` (lib.c:785).\n4. `>=` vs `==` comparisons on `mode`: lib.c:560, 590, 713 use `mode >= STBDS_HM_STRING`, but lib.c:836 and lib.c:842 use `mode == STBDS_HM_STRING`. Confirm the Rust preserves each one exactly.\n5. Truncating casts: lib.c:803 `h->string.mode = (unsigned char) mode;` and lib.c:825 `int i = slot & STBDS_BUCKET_MASK;`.\n6. Wrapping vs panicking arithmetic: any place the C relies on unsigned wraparound (e.g. `2 * stbds_arrcap(a)`, `stbds_hash_seed * a + b`, `len << (STBDS_SIZE_T_BITS-8)`, `hash += 2`, `--table->tombstone_count`) where the Rust uses a non-wrapping operator that could panic in debug builds.\n7. Shift amounts: `STBDS_ROTATE_LEFT/RIGHT` (lib.c:474-475) and `512u << (a->block>>1)` at lib.c:888 — could the Rust shift by >= 64 and panic where the C is merely UB-but-benign?\n8. Any C statement with NO Rust counterpart at all, or vice versa.\n9. `helxo` (lib.c:945-963): the C does `printf(\"%s %c\\n\", hash[z], hash[z].value)` passing a 16-byte STRUCT `{char* key; char value;}` by value as the first variadic argument. Verify the Rust's reproduction is ABI-equivalent on x86-64 SysV.\n\nFor every divergence report: the C line number, the exact C expression, the Rust line number, the exact Rust expression, and a concrete input that would make them differ. If a construct looks different but is provably equivalent, say \"equivalent\" and give the one-line reason. Be precise and terse; no summaries of what the code does.","uuid":"68c34ec2-27b5-49ac-9baf-46515ce29b8b","session_id":"e2dcfccc-b98f-4c68-871b-22e93296d372"}
